  "content": "# Implementing a Bounded LRU Cache in Python\n\n## Overview\n\nA **Least Recently Used (LRU) cache** is a data structure that evicts the least recently used item when the cache reaches its capacity. This is a common pattern in systems where memory is limited but access patterns are predictable (e.g., database connection pools, HTTP caches, file system caches).\n\nPython's `collections.OrderedDict` provides an ideal foundation for an LRU cache because it maintains insertion order and supports moving items to the end in O(1) time.\n\n## Core Design\n\nThe key insight is that `OrderedDict` preserves insertion order, and we can use `move_to_end()` to update an item's position when it is accessed. When the cache is full and we need to insert a new item, we remove the first item (the least recently used one).\n\n### Class Implementation\n\n```python\nfrom collections import OrderedDict\n\n\nclass LRUCache:\n    \"\"\"\n    A bounded LRU cache using OrderedDict.\n    \n    The cache maintains a fixed maximum size. When the cache is full\n    and a new item is added, the least recently used item is evicted.\n    \n    Both get() and put() operations run in O(1) average time.\n    \"\"\"\n\n    def __init__(self, capacity: int):\n        \"\"\"\n        Initialize the LRU cache with a given capacity.\n        \n        Args:\n            capacity: Maximum number of items the cache can hold.\n                       Must be a positive integer.\n        \n        Raises:\n            ValueError: If capacity is less than 1.\n        \"\"\"\n        if capacity < 1:\n            raise ValueError(\"Capacity must be at least 1\")\n        \n        self.capacity = capacity\n        self.cache = OrderedDict()\n\n    def get(self, key) -> object:\n        \"\"\"\n        Retrieve the value associated with the given key.\n        \n        If the key exists, it is moved to the most recently used position.\n        If the key does not exist, returns None.\n        \n        Args:\n            key: The key to look up.\n        \n        Returns:\n            The value associated with the key, or None if not found.\n        \"\"\"\n        if key not in self.cache:\n            return None\n        \n        # Move the accessed key to the end (most recently used)\n        self.cache.move_to_end(key)\n        return self.cache[key]\n\n    def",
